--TEST--
`Core\Uri::buildQuery` renders each value by the query string's own rule rather than by `as string`, and the divergences are exactly the two it is written around: `false` is `0` and a `null` drops its pair — spec § 12, `rule:types/conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The three neighbouring cases assert `buildQuery` against `parseQuery` — the
// inverse, the fixed point, the bracket convention. None of them can see what
// this one asks: whether a value's *text* is the conversion `rule:types/conversion`
// already defines. It is not, and `scalar_text` in uri.rs says why — a query
// string has no spelling for an absent value, and `false as string` is the
// empty string, which on the wire is indistinguishable from `""`.
//
// So the whole table is swept and the agreements are **counted**: a member
// that quietly reached for `as string` would print nine plausible lines and
// fail on the two that matter.
class Box {
    public int $n = 1;
}

array<mixed> $values = [1, 0, -7, 3.5, 0.0, true, false, "a b", "", "1", null, [1, 2]];

int $dropped = 0;
int $agreed = 0;
int $diverged = 0;

foreach ($values as mixed $v) {
    array<mixed> $one = ["k" => $v];
    string $built = Core\Uri::buildQuery($one);

    // `as string` from a `mixed` is the checked conversion, so the nested
    // array's row is a throw rather than a rendering — that is the third kind
    // of value here, and the reason the comparison is guarded.
    tainted string $direct = "<throws>";
    try {
        $direct = $v as string;
    } catch (Throwable $e) {
        $direct = "<throws>";
    }

    // Agreement is `k=` plus the form encoding of what `as string` answered:
    // the encoder is not what is under test here, so it is applied to both
    // sides rather than asserted on one.
    string $verdict = "";
    if ($built == "") {
        $dropped = $dropped + 1;
        $verdict = "dropped";
    } else {
        if ($direct != "<throws>" && $built == "k=" . Core\Uri::encodeFormValue($direct)) {
            $agreed = $agreed + 1;
            $verdict = "agrees";
        } else {
            $diverged = $diverged + 1;
            $verdict = "diverges";
        }
    }

    echo "[", $built, "] as-string=[", $direct, "] ", $verdict, "\n";
}

// `null` is the row worth reading twice: `as string` has a spelling for it and
// the wire does not, so the pair goes rather than being written empty — which
// is what keeps `parseQuery(buildQuery($a))` an inverse.
echo "dropped=", $dropped, " agreed=", $agreed, " diverged=", $diverged, "\n";

// `scalar_text`'s own throw, and it is a `Fault::thrown`, so a handler reaches
// it: a value that is neither a scalar nor a nested array has no query text at
// all, and the member says so rather than writing something.
try {
    echo Core\Uri::buildQuery(["k" => new Box()]), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $o) {
    echo "an object has no query text\n";
}
--EXPECT--
[k=1] as-string=[1] agrees
[k=0] as-string=[0] agrees
[k=-7] as-string=[-7] agrees
[k=3.5] as-string=[3.5] agrees
[k=0] as-string=[0] agrees
[k=1] as-string=[1] agrees
[k=0] as-string=[] diverges
[k=a+b] as-string=[a b] agrees
[k=] as-string=[] agrees
[k=1] as-string=[1] agrees
[] as-string=[] dropped
[k%5B0%5D=1&k%5B1%5D=2] as-string=[<throws>] diverges
dropped=1 agreed=9 diverged=2
an object has no query text
